/* Results interpretation. Not "do you remember the number" — "can you read it".
 *
 * Every answer here is written to the boundary standard on the Boundary page:
 * a claim, the arithmetic behind it, the unit of inference, what it supports,
 * what it does not support and under which named failure mode. Where an
 * interval cannot exist, the answer says so instead of hedging.
 *
 * Statistics are computed from the means and standard deviations published on
 * our own pages. Welch intervals; minimum detectable effect at alpha 0.05
 * two-sided and power 0.80. Every one of them treats seedlings as independent,
 * which is the pseudoreplication we declare in the same breath.
 */
window.QA_RESULTS = [

/* ── the ladder ── */
{id:'RS01',kind:'results',topic:'ladder',lvl:1,
 q_en:'Take me through your salt ladder figure. What does it establish?',
 q_zh:'帶我看你們的鹽度梯度圖。它建立了什麼？',
 claim:'The assay resolves a monotone dose response in primary root length between 0 and 150 mM NaCl, in Arabidopsis on agar, at day 6.',
 moves:['Four doses, nine measured seedlings each, day 6: 74.95, 34.77, 11.00 and 1.36 mm.',
        'Monotone, and the 95 per cent intervals of the means do not overlap, so the assay separates adjacent doses.',
        'That is what it establishes, and it is the only clean dose response in the project. It sets the stress window for every rescue experiment and it is the injury calibration the model consumes.'],
 stat:'95% CI of each mean: 72.82–77.08, 31.38–38.16, 7.87–14.13, 0.52–2.20 mm. Coefficient of variation rises across the ladder: 4%, 13%, 37%, 80%.',
 unit:'Nine seedlings on one plate per dose. The observational unit is the seedling; the experimental unit is the plate, and there is one plate per dose.',
 holds:['The order of the four doses, and that the assay is not blind to plant condition.',
        'A stress window: 75 mM leaves 46 per cent of control, 100 mM leaves 15 per cent.',
        'A fractional injury curve the model can use, because the model takes a proportion and not a millimetre.'],
 fails:['A dose–response fit. Four points, one plate each, and the top of the range is a floor — not enough to fit a curve. (Floor effect, no replication.)',
        'Anything about a protectant. No treatment is in this figure.',
        'Any transfer to soil or to a crop. Soil needed a completely different schedule, 100 then 200 then 300 mM with no water between.'],
 ev:'Set 6. Vernalised 19 July, into the incubator 23 July, measured 3 August. ImageJ, traced by hand.',
 trap:'Do not say "significant dose response". Say the intervals do not overlap, then say the plate is the unit. The first half is the strength and the second half is why it is credible.',
 lit:['munns08','hurlbert84']},

{id:'RS02',kind:'results',topic:'ladder',lvl:3,
 q_en:'Your Results figure says the fall is 42.7-fold. Your Plants page says about 55. Reconcile them for me, now.',
 q_zh:'Results圖說下降42.7倍，Plants頁說約55倍。現在就把它對起來。',
 claim:'Both numbers come from the same measurements with different denominators, and the arithmetic closes to within rounding. The error is quoting a ratio at all.',
 moves:['74.95 divided by 1.36 is 55.1. That uses all nine seedlings at 150 mM.',
        'The nine roots sum to 12.24 mm. Drop the two that were not measurable and the mean of seven is 1.749 mm, which gives 42.9. The legend says 42.7.',
        'So neither page is wrong about the data. What is wrong is the statistic: the denominator has a coefficient of variation of 80 per cent, so the fold change is only known to within about a factor of three.',
        'We should have published the two means in both places and dropped the ratio.'],
 stat:'Ratio 55.1, 95% CI 32.6 to 93.1 fold (delta method on the log scale). A legend of 42.7 implies a denominator of 1.755 mm; seven of the nine roots give 1.749 mm.',
 unit:'Nine seedlings on one plate at each dose; the ratio inherits the uncertainty of both means and is dominated by the smaller one.',
 holds:['That the fall is large and that our two pages are internally consistent once the denominator is named.',
        'That we can audit our own figures in public.'],
 fails:['A point value for the fold change. Any single number implies a precision the denominator cannot carry. (Ratio with an unstable denominator.)'],
 ev:'Plants dry lab section against Results figure 13 legend.',
 trap:'Do not say "that is our error of presentation" and stop. Show the seven-seedling arithmetic. A reconciliation beats an apology, and this one takes fifteen seconds.',
 lit:['nakagawa07']},

{id:'RS03',kind:'results',topic:'ladder',lvl:2,
 q_en:'Why do you not run rescue experiments at 150 mM, where the stress is clearest?',
 q_zh:'逆境在150 mM最明顯，為什麼不在那裡做救援實驗？',
 claim:'At 150 mM the readout has reached its floor: the mean is 1.36 mm and the standard deviation is 1.09, so the noise and the signal are the same size.',
 moves:['The mean is 1.36 mm with a spread of 1.09, a coefficient of variation of 80 per cent. Two of the nine roots could not be measured at all.',
        'The smallest difference this design could resolve there is 1.34 mm, which is as large as the mean. So a protectant would have to roughly double a barely growing root before we could see it.',
        'That is why the screening window is 75 to 100 mM, and why almost everything is run at 100.'],
 stat:'150 mM: 1.36 ± 1.09 mm, n = 9, 95% CI 0.52–2.20. Minimum detectable difference 1.34 mm at power 0.80. At 100 mM the mean is 11.00 ± 4.07 and the detectable difference is 6.43 mm.',
 unit:'Seedlings on a shared plate. The floor also makes the measurement itself unreliable, because unmeasurable roots are dropped and that shifts the mean upward.',
 holds:['The choice of a 75–100 mM screening window, as a design decision with a number behind it.'],
 fails:['Any claim that a protectant does or does not work at 150 mM. The design could not have seen it either way. (Floor effect plus minimum detectable effect.)'],
 ev:'Plants §1.3. Set 5 showed 250 mM kills before a protectant can act; both advisors capped the useful range at 200 mM.',
 trap:'The temptation is to say "the stress is too severe". Severity is not the problem — the floor on the readout is. Say the number.',
 lit:['halsey25']},

/* ── trehalose ── */
{id:'RS04',kind:'results',topic:'treh',lvl:2,
 q_en:'Your trehalose arm gained 3.71 millimetres at 100 millimolar. Is that real?',
 q_zh:'海藻糖在100 mM下多了3.71毫米。那是真的嗎？',
 claim:'The estimate is +3.71 mm with a 95 per cent interval from −1.18 to +8.60, so it cannot be separated from zero and cannot be ruled out either.',
 moves:['The interval crosses zero, so there is no effect to report.',
        'More useful: the smallest difference this design could have resolved at 100 mM is 6.43 mm. The observed difference is below that, so even a genuine 4 mm effect would have been invisible here.',
        'So the correct statement is not "trehalose did nothing". It is that this design cannot distinguish nothing from about 5 millimetres.',
        'And all of that treats the nine seedlings as independent, which they are not — they share a plate.'],
 stat:'+3.71 mm, SE 2.30, Welch df 14.7, t = 1.62, 95% CI −1.18 to +8.60, Hedges g 0.73. Pooled SD 4.87 mm, minimum detectable difference 6.43 mm.',
 unit:'One plate per condition. With the plate as the unit there is no within-arm variance at all, so none of the above is formally admissible — it is the most favourable analysis available, and it still shows nothing.',
 holds:['That no rescue is claimable from this contrast.',
        'That the design, not the molecule, is what the null is about.'],
 fails:['"Trehalose does not protect Arabidopsis." That is a claim about trehalose; our data is a claim about our power. (Pseudoreplication plus underpowered design.)'],
 ev:'Results §6.2, set 6.',
 trap:'Giving the interval and stopping is already better than most answers. Giving the detectable difference is what makes it a graduate answer.',
 lit:['nakagawa07','halsey25','hurlbert84']},

{id:'RS05',kind:'results',topic:'treh',lvl:3,
 q_en:'Run the numbers on your own 150 millimolar trehalose pair. That one is significant, is it not?',
 q_zh:'把你們150 mM海藻糖那一對的數字算一下。那一組是顯著的吧？',
 claim:'It reaches the usual threshold nominally and it should still not be reported, for three reasons that each stand alone.',
 moves:['The numbers first: 2.53 against 1.36 mm, so +1.17 mm, t = 2.44, nominal p about 0.015 at the seedling level.',
        'First reason: four contrasts were run on this set. A Bonferroni threshold is 0.0125, and 0.015 does not clear it. We did not pre-specify a primary contrast.',
        'Second: the unit is the plate and there is one plate. A test at the seedling level is pseudoreplication, and it is the reason the interval looks as tight as it does.',
        'Third: the effect is +1.17 mm on roots whose mean is 1.36 mm, inside the floor of the assay, where unmeasurable roots were dropped from one arm and not the other.'],
 stat:'+1.17 mm, SE 0.48, t = 2.44, nominal p ≈ 0.015, 95% CI +0.15 to +2.19, Hedges g 1.09. Four contrasts in the set; Bonferroni alpha 0.0125. Minimum detectable difference at this dose 1.34 mm.',
 unit:'Nine seedlings, one plate. The p-value is computed on observational units, so it answers a question nobody asked: whether these nine seedlings differ from those nine.',
 holds:['Nothing. This is the one contrast in the set that looks publishable and it is the one most likely to be an artefact.'],
 fails:['A rescue at 150 mM. (Multiplicity, pseudoreplication, floor effect — any one of the three is sufficient.)'],
 ev:'Plants §1.3, the trehalose column: 71.96, 29.18, 14.71, 2.53 mm with SD 3.13, 8.95, 5.56, 0.94.',
 trap:'If a judge hands you a significant-looking number from your own data, the test is whether you will take it. Do not take it. Name the three reasons and move on.',
 lit:['hurlbert84','lazic18']},

{id:'RS06',kind:'results',topic:'treh',lvl:3,
 q_en:'Across your whole trehalose programme, which single comparison is best resolved?',
 q_zh:'整個海藻糖計畫裡，哪一個比較分辨得最清楚？',
 claim:'The unstressed arm, and it shows a cost: 5 mM trehalose on plants with no salt gave 2.99 mm less root than untreated.',
 moves:['At 0 mM the difference is −2.99 mm with an interval from −5.94 to −0.04, and the detectable difference there is 3.90 mm. It is the tightest contrast in the set because the unstressed spread is small.',
        'Set 1 says the same thing more loudly: 1 mM trehalose on unstressed seedlings gave 14.6 mm against 51.7 mm untreated.',
        'So the clearest thing six sets of trehalose produced is evidence that an added osmolyte costs something when the plant does not need it. That is a real finding, and it is the one our advisor asks about when he raises the growth trade-off.'],
 stat:'0 mM: −2.99 mm, SE 1.39, t = −2.15, 95% CI −5.94 to −0.04, Hedges g −0.96, minimum detectable difference 3.90 mm. Set 1 unstressed: 14.6 vs 51.7 mm.',
 unit:'One plate per condition again, so the direction is better supported than the magnitude.',
 holds:['That the unstressed arm is where our data is strongest, and that it points at a cost.',
        'That our assay is not insensitive — it resolved something, just not the thing we wanted.'],
 fails:['A quantitative cost. The interval nearly touches zero and the unit is still the plate. (Pseudoreplication.)'],
 ev:'Plants §1.3 and set 1.',
 trap:'Most teams skip their no-stress arms. Leading with the arm that makes the protectant look bad is what shows a judge you read your own data instead of harvesting it.',
 lit:['monson21','he22']},

{id:'RS07',kind:'results',topic:'treh',lvl:3,
 q_en:'You say trehalose did not work. Make that a claim I can assess.',
 q_zh:'你們說海藻糖沒效。把它變成一個我可以評估的主張。',
 claim:'In six sets we did not resolve a rescue, and we can state the size of effect we would have missed.',
 moves:['The design-level statement: at the three salt doses the smallest differences we could have resolved are 9.32, 6.43 and 1.34 millimetres. None of the observed differences reaches its own threshold.',
        'The reproducibility statement: set 3 ran two trials of the same design and they disagreed, and set 5 picked 5 mM in one trial and 10 mM in the other.',
        'The honest form: we are not claiming no effect, we are claiming no resolved effect with a power statement attached. To claim no effect we would need an equivalence test against a pre-set margin, and that needs independent plates.',
        'And the one thing we did resolve points the other way — a cost in the unstressed arm.'],
 stat:'Minimum detectable differences: 3.90 mm at 0, 9.32 at 75, 6.43 at 100, 1.34 at 150. Observed |differences|: 2.99, 5.59, 3.71, 1.17 mm.',
 unit:'Six sets, each with one plate per condition. Across sets the plates are independent, so the sets are closer to replicates than the seedlings are — but the doses and designs changed between them, so they cannot be pooled.',
 holds:['A negative result with its sensitivity stated, which is a result.',
        'The decision to drop trehalose from the circuit design in August.'],
 fails:['"Trehalose is not protective." Published work reports protection in maize and Arabidopsis at 10 mM. Our dose window, our species and our stress levels all differ. (No equivalence test; external validity.)'],
 ev:'Sets 1–6, Results §6.2. Published protection at 10 mM in maize (Rohman 2019) and in Arabidopsis alongside an ACC deaminase strain (Kim 2025).',
 trap:'"It did not work in our hands" is the correct scope and it is also the honest one. Never make it a statement about the molecule.',
 lit:['halsey25','nakagawa07']},

/* ── the commercial peptide ── */
{id:'RS08',kind:'results',topic:'chpep',lvl:2,
 q_en:'Three and a half times the control. Tell me what that number means.',
 q_zh:'對照的三點五倍。告訴我這個數字是什麼意思。',
 claim:'It means chlorophyll under treatment came back to about four fifths of the unstressed plate. The 3.5 figure is the same data divided by the damaged control instead.',
 moves:['Against the salted control: 0.2398 and 0.2564 over 0.0690, so 3.48 and 3.72 times.',
        'Against the unsalted control on the same plate: over 0.2986, so 0.80 and 0.86 times.',
        'Both are arithmetically right. The second describes the plant — treatment held chlorophyll near, and still below, the unstressed level. The first describes the distance from the damaged arm, which is not what a reader hears.',
        'And in the same breath: that plate has a broken internal control, and there is one plate per arm.'],
 stat:'Control ladder on the plate: 0.2986, 0.2479, 0.0690 at 0, 75, 100 mM — the 100 mM control is 0.23 of unsalted. Treated arms: 3.48× and 3.72× the salted control, 0.80× and 0.86× the unsalted control. Three reads per arm are technical replicates of one extract.',
 unit:'One plate per arm. The three replicates measure the plate reader and the pipette, not the biology.',
 holds:['That the assay can register a large difference at all, which no other dataset of ours shows.',
        'That the control ladder on this plate behaves, so the plate was not a total failure.'],
 fails:['A fold-change claim. The denominator is a choice and we would be choosing the flattering one. (Ratio denominator.)',
        'A rescue claim. One plate per arm, and one arm\'s no-salt control read below every salted arm. (No biological replication; internal control failure.)'],
 ev:'Results §6.2, trial 4, 5 September. 95% acetone at 649 and 665 nm with Lichtenthaler coefficients.',
 trap:'The 3.5 is the most attractive number in the project and it is a denominator artefact dressed as a result. Give both ratios in one sentence, every time.',
 lit:['nakagawa07']},

{id:'RS09',kind:'results',topic:'chpep',lvl:3,
 q_en:'One arm on that plate has a no-salt control reading below every salted arm. What happened?',
 q_zh:'那一皿上有一組的無鹽對照低於所有加鹽組。發生了什麼事？',
 claim:'We do not know which failure it was, and we can name the candidates and say which ones are separable.',
 moves:['The number: 0.0318, which is 0.11 of the main no-salt control and 0.46 of the 100 mM control. A healthy unstressed plate cannot read below a salt-damaged one, so this is a measurement or a handling failure.',
        'Four candidates. An extraction that went wrong, which is the most likely because the same project has a documented ten-fold spread within one extract. A sample swap between wells. A transposed column pair, which is documented in our heat sheet. Or a plate-position effect in the incubator.',
        'Which are separable: an extraction failure and a swap are distinguishable by re-extracting the stored tissue, if it exists. A transposition is testable on the raw OD columns. A position effect is not recoverable after the fact.',
        'What it costs: the plate loses its reference, so every arm on it loses its comparison, not only this one.'],
 stat:'0.0318 against a main no-salt control of 0.2986 and a 100 mM control of 0.0690. Precedent in the same project: set 8 gave 0.106, 0.830 and 1.092 from one extract, and two workbooks differ on those rows by 7 to 66 fold.',
 unit:'The plate. An internal control failure is a plate-level event, which is exactly why one plate per arm is expensive.',
 holds:['That we found it ourselves and published it next to the attractive number.'],
 fails:['Any use of the trial 4 table as evidence of rescue. (Internal control failure.)'],
 ev:'Results §6.2 and §6.5.',
 trap:'"We are not sure" is weak. Four named candidates with the test that separates each one is the same honesty with a method attached.',
 lit:['lazic18']},

{id:'RS10',kind:'results',topic:'chpep',lvl:2,
 q_en:'You have a photograph that looks convincing and a table that does not hold. Which is your evidence?',
 q_zh:'你們有一張看起來很有說服力的照片，和一張站不住的表。哪一個是你們的證據？',
 claim:'Neither on its own. The photograph is an observation and the table is a failed effect estimate, and we publish them at those two levels.',
 moves:['The photograph is set 7 trial 2 at day 12: at 100 mM the co- and after-treatment plates are dense and green beside a sparse untreated one. That is a real observation with a date on it.',
        'A photograph cannot be an effect estimate. It has no scale, the plates were not randomised in position, and an observer who knows the labels is scoring it.',
        'The table is trial 4, and it fails on its own internal control. So the strongest honest sentence is: we observed a large difference and we have not measured one.',
        'That is why the Plants page describes what we saw and the Results page applies the standard for a claim. The two pages are held to different bars on purpose.'],
 stat:'Trial 1 of the same set lost both pre-treatment plates to contamination, so that trial cannot rank pre-treatment at all.',
 unit:'One plate per arm in both the photographed trial and the measured one.',
 holds:['An observation at a date, on a named plate, which is the first rung of the claim ladder.'],
 fails:['A protectant effect. A picture plus a broken table does not add to an estimate. (No quantification; unblinded scoring.)'],
 ev:'Plants §4.1 set 7 against Results §6.2 and §9.',
 trap:'This is the likeliest hostile pairing in the session, because both sentences are ours. Answer with the two rungs of the ladder, not with a defence of either page.',
 lit:['nakagawa07']},

{id:'RS11',kind:'results',topic:'chpep',lvl:3,
 q_en:'Your whole hardware case rests on timing. What do four timing trials tell you about timing?',
 q_zh:'你們整個硬體論述建立在時機上。四次時機試驗告訴你們關於時機的什麼？',
 claim:'That the question is answerable with this assay and that we have not answered it. Four runs gave three orderings.',
 moves:['What happened: at 75 mM co-treatment looked best, at 100 mM after-treatment looked best, one trial lost both pre-treatment plates, and the hydroponic sets ranked them differently again depending on the normalisation.',
        'An inconsistent ranking across four runs with one unit each is what you expect when the between-plate variance is larger than the effect and nobody has measured the between-plate variance.',
        'So we report no timing result, and our own page says there is not enough data to fit a timing model.',
        'The design is still right, and it is the thing worth keeping: three timings against one stress with three plates per cell is a 9-plate experiment that would settle it.'],
 stat:'Four trials, three orderings, one experimental unit per arm in each. No between-plate variance estimate exists anywhere in the project.',
 unit:'Plate, or box, with k = 1 per arm. With k = 1 a ranking carries no uncertainty estimate, so inconsistency between runs is uninformative about the truth and informative about the design.',
 holds:['That the timing arm is the experiment that decides whether a forecast-triggered reactor is worth building.',
        'That we ran it four times and said what came back.'],
 fails:['Any ordering of pre, co and after treatment. (k = 1 per arm; no variance component.)',
        'Using the plant data to support the model\'s timing assumption. The plants calibrated injury, nothing else.'],
 ev:'Plants §1.3, §7.1, set 7, Results §6.4.',
 trap:'Do not defend the model with data it does not have. Separate what is calibrated from what is assumed, and say which arm would close it.',
 lit:['hurlbert84']},

/* ── hydroponics ── */
{id:'RS12',kind:'results',topic:'hydro',lvl:2,
 q_en:'Your two normalisations give opposite rankings on the same four samples. Explain it.',
 q_zh:'同樣四個樣本，兩種標準化給出相反的排序。解釋一下。',
 claim:'The two columns differ by the fresh mass, and the mass is itself the outcome. Dividing by it puts the result into the denominator.',
 moves:['Per gram of fresh weight the untreated box ranks first at 0.0828 and co-treatment last at 0.0617, a span of 1.34-fold. Per plant, co-treatment ranks first at 1.251 and the untreated box falls to third at 0.714, a span of 3.39-fold.',
        'Divide the two columns and you recover the mass: about 8.6 milligrams per plant untreated, about 20.3 for co-treatment. A 2.4-fold mass difference reverses a 1.34-fold span easily.',
        'Why the untreated box is lightest: it was the most damaged. So per-gram normalisation rewards the worst arm.',
        'And there was one box per arm, so neither column has an interval. There is nothing to rank in either direction.'],
 stat:'Per gram: 0.0828 / 0.0782 / 0.0734 / 0.0617. Per plant: 1.251 / 0.997 / 0.714 / 0.369. Recorded pooled fresh weights span 0.0503 to 0.2029 g. Implied mass per plant: 0.714/0.0828 → 8.6 mg untreated; 1.251/0.0617 → 20.3 mg co-treatment.',
 unit:'One box per arm, one reading per box. Within-arm variance is undefined, so no standard error, no interval and no test exist for either column.',
 holds:['A measurement lesson we can hand to another team, with the arithmetic attached.',
        'That fresh weight should be reported as an outcome in its own right.'],
 fails:['Any ranking of the timings. (k = 1 per arm.)',
        'Any per-gram comparison between arms whose mass differs. (Ratio denominator that is itself the outcome.)'],
 ev:'Results §6.4. Salinity set 1: 100 mM on 28 July, 13-day-old plants, four boxes, read 4 August.',
 trap:'Do the division out loud. The flip stops being a puzzle the moment the mass is on the table, and doing the arithmetic in the room is worth more than describing it.',
 lit:['nakagawa07']},

{id:'RS13',kind:'results',topic:'hydro',lvl:3,
 q_en:'A reviewer tells you to pick one normalisation and publish it. Which?',
 q_zh:'審稿人要你們選一種標準化發表。選哪個？',
 claim:'Per plant, with fresh weight printed beside it as its own outcome — and with the statement that neither licenses a ranking.',
 moves:['Per plant, because the denominator is a count fixed by the design and not changed by the treatment. Per gram divides an outcome by an outcome.',
        'Fresh weight goes next to it, because it is arguably the most integrative readout we have and hiding it in a denominator wastes it.',
        'But the answer to the reviewer is that the choice does not matter here: with one box per arm there is no interval on either column, so we would report both and claim neither.',
        'If the reviewer insists on a single ranked figure, the right response is to run it again with three boxes per arm, not to choose a column.'],
 stat:'Per-gram span 1.34-fold; per-plant span 3.39-fold; implied mass span 2.4-fold between the two arms whose rank is stated.',
 unit:'Box, k = 1.',
 holds:['A reporting rule another team can adopt: name the denominator, and never use a denominator the treatment moved.'],
 fails:['The ranking itself, under either column.'],
 ev:'Results §6.4.',
 trap:'Picking the flattering column and not saying so is the failure this question hunts for. Give the reason for the choice, then say it still does not license a claim.',
 lit:['nakagawa07']},

{id:'RS14',kind:'results',topic:'hydro',lvl:2,
 q_en:'There is no unstressed box in that hydroponic set. What does that cost you?',
 q_zh:'那一組水耕沒有無逆境的箱子。這讓你們損失了什麼？',
 claim:'It removes the reference every readout needs, so the set can only compare treatments with each other and cannot say whether any of them protected anything.',
 moves:['Every arm in that set is at 100 mM. The "control" is the untreated salted box, so the comparison is treatment against no treatment under stress, with no healthy baseline.',
        'Without an unstressed box there is no way to express a result as a fraction of normal, which is the only form our model can use and the only form that transfers between experiments.',
        'It also removes the check that caught the problem in trial 4: an unstressed control that reads too low is how you find a broken plate.',
        'We fixed this later by moving the dose into the plate from set 8 and by always running a matched unstressed arm, which is what the stress index is defined against.'],
 stat:'Four boxes, arms three days apart, no unstressed box, read 4 August. The stress index is defined as 1.0 at the matched unstressed control, so this set cannot produce an index value at all.',
 unit:'Box, k = 1, and no reference level.',
 holds:['Nothing about protection. The set is a design lesson.'],
 fails:['Any statement in fractional terms, including anything the model could consume. (No reference level.)'],
 ev:'Plants §2.2, Results §6.4, Plants §7.2 for the index definition.',
 trap:'Do not let this be absorbed into the general n = 1 answer. A missing baseline is a different fault from missing replication, and naming it separately shows you know which is which.'},

{id:'RS15',kind:'results',topic:'hydro',lvl:3,
 q_en:'What was actually in those boxes?',
 q_zh:'那些箱子裡到底加了什麼？',
 claim:'The run sheet names the CH Biotech peptide; the data archive contains no file that names the agent. We publish the second position.',
 moves:['Our Plants page states the protectant was the CH Bio peptide, taken from that run\'s own timeline sheet. Our Results page states that no file in the archive names what went into the boxes.',
        'Both sentences are ours and they are about different documents, so the honest answer names both and says which one governs.',
        'The archive governs, because provenance is part of a claim. An agent identified only on a planning sheet cannot be attached to a measurement made a week later.',
        'So we do not publish this set as a protectant result in either direction.'],
 stat:'One box per arm, one reading per box, and no agent identification in the measurement archive.',
 unit:'Box, k = 1, with unresolved provenance — which is a prior problem to replication, not a lesser one.',
 holds:['The normalisation lesson, which does not depend on what the agent was.'],
 fails:['Everything else about the set. (Provenance.)'],
 ev:'Plants §2.2 against Results §6.4.',
 trap:'Do not resolve the conflict in the room by picking the page that helps. Name both documents, say the archive governs, and say what that costs the set.'},

/* ── heat ── */
{id:'RS16',kind:'results',topic:'heat',lvl:3,
 q_en:'You withdrew a chlorophyll result. How do you know the columns were transposed and the peptide did not simply halve chlorophyll?',
 q_zh:'你們撤回了一筆葉綠素資料。你們怎麼知道是欄位錯置，而不是胜肽真的讓葉綠素腰斬？',
 claim:'Because three different application timings produced the same value, and that is a signature of the measurement and not of a treatment.',
 moves:['The numbers: control 0.626 mg/g, and the three timings at 0.348, 0.337 and 0.335. All three land at about 54 per cent of control and within four per cent of each other.',
        'A real timing effect varies with the timing. Three arms applied on three different days converging on one value is what a column error looks like.',
        'Then the direct check: in the control row the OD645 and OD663 columns appear transposed relative to every treated row. The two wavelengths carry different coefficients, so swapping them shifts the computed value by about the factor observed.',
        'And the project has precedent: set 8 produced 0.106, 0.830 and 1.092 from one extract, with two workbooks differing on those rows by seven to sixty-six fold. Spreadsheet handling is a known failure mode here, not a speculative one.'],
 stat:'0.348, 0.337, 0.335 against 0.626 mg/g: 55.6%, 53.8%, 53.5% of control, a 2.1% range across three arms. Chlorophyll a and b coefficients at 645 and 663 nm differ, so a column swap is not a symmetric error.',
 unit:'One extract per arm with technical replicates. A transposition is a row-level error and it would survive any amount of replication, which is why replication is not the fix.',
 holds:['The decision to withdraw, and the reason for it, which is stated on our own page.'],
 fails:['Any heat chlorophyll result, in either direction. (Suspected data-handling error, unresolved.)'],
 ev:'Results §6.3, Plants heat pretest. Also §6.5 for the set 8 precedent.',
 trap:'Never quote "the peptide halved chlorophyll", and never defend the withdrawal as caution. It was withdrawn for a reason you can state in one sentence. State it.'},

{id:'RS17',kind:'results',topic:'heat',lvl:2,
 q_en:'In your heat pretest every treated arm did worse than the control. Is your protectant harmful under heat?',
 q_zh:'你們熱逆境預試裡，每個處理組都比對照差。保護劑在熱逆境下是有害的嗎？',
 claim:'We cannot say, and the pretest is not the design that would answer it.',
 moves:['The numbers un-normalised: 35.5 for the control against 20.2, 17.3 and 16.4 for the three timings. The direction is consistent across the three arms, which is the only thing that makes it worth saying at all.',
        'Why it is not a result: five days at 37 degrees with no recovery phase and weak chamber light. Heat injury is a duration plus a recovery, and without the recovery there is nothing to recover.',
        'One plate per arm, and three of the eight plates in the parallel run carried contamination. So the between-plate variance is not merely unmeasured, it is known to be large.',
        'To claim harm we would need the same equivalence machinery as a claim of benefit, against a pre-set margin, with independent plates.'],
 stat:'35.5 vs 20.2, 17.3, 16.4 — treated arms at 57%, 49% and 46% of control, with k = 1 per arm. No interval exists.',
 unit:'Plate, k = 1. Three arms pointing the same way is suggestive and is not replication, because they share the same control plate.',
 holds:['That we call it a pretest and publish it as one.',
        'That no treated arm beat the control, said as an observation.'],
 fails:['Harm. (No replication, no recovery phase, no equivalence margin.)',
        'Benefit. The same design cannot support either.'],
 ev:'Plants heat pretest, Results §6.3.',
 trap:'An inconvenient direction is tempting to over-concede. The honest position is symmetric: this design supports neither harm nor benefit, and we say so in both directions.',
 lit:['halsey25','agathokleous20']},

{id:'RS18',kind:'results',topic:'heat',lvl:2,
 q_en:'Your hydroponic heat set ranks untreated first on one normalisation and pre-treatment first on the other. Again?',
 q_zh:'水耕熱逆境那組，一種標準化是未處理第一，另一種是前處理第一。又來了？',
 claim:'Yes, and the second time it is not a surprise — it is the same denominator problem in a second platform, which is why we treat the first instance as a rule and not an anecdote.',
 moves:['Untreated reads highest per gram of fresh weight; pre-treatment reads highest per plant. One box per arm again.',
        'The mechanism is identical to the salinity set: the heavier arm is penalised per gram, and mass is set by the treatment.',
        'Two independent platforms producing the same reversal is the strongest form this observation takes. It raises the normalisation point from a quirk of one dataset to a property of the readout.',
        'What we changed because of it: chlorophyll is reported per plant with mass alongside, and the stress index is computed from raw values and not from already-normalised ones.'],
 stat:'Two platforms, same reversal, k = 1 per arm in both. Salinity set span: 1.34-fold per gram against 3.39-fold per plant.',
 unit:'Box, k = 1.',
 holds:['A reporting rule, now supported in two platforms.'],
 fails:['Any timing ranking under heat. (k = 1 per arm, ratio denominator.)'],
 ev:'Plants §2.2, Results §6.4.',
 trap:'Do not present the repeat as a second failure. Two instances of one mechanism is the evidence that the mechanism is real, and that is the only place this data is strong.'},

/* ── our own proteins ── */
{id:'RS19',kind:'results',topic:'ours',lvl:2,
 q_en:'Set 8 gave 0.106, 0.830 and 1.092 from one extract. What kind of error is that?',
 q_zh:'第8組同一份萃取液讀出0.106、0.830、1.092。那是什麼樣的錯誤？',
 claim:'A measurement failure, not a biological one. Three reads of one extract should differ by a few per cent, and these differ ten-fold.',
 moves:['What those three numbers are: technical replicates. They estimate the pipette and the plate reader. Nothing biological can vary between them, because it is the same liquid.',
        'A ten-fold spread therefore means the reading is not a measurement. Five of twelve rows look like this.',
        'And then the transcription layer: two workbooks differ on those rows by factors of seven to sixty-six. So the error is in handling as well as in reading.',
        'Which is why set 8 has no published number at all, and why the set is marked void instead of being averaged.'],
 stat:'0.106, 0.830, 1.092 from one extract: a 10.3-fold spread where a few per cent is expected. Five of twelve rows affected; cross-workbook discrepancies of 7 to 66 fold.',
 unit:'Technical replicates of one observational unit. Averaging them would hide the failure and produce a number with a tight-looking standard error.',
 holds:['The decision to void the set.'],
 fails:['Every number in it. (Technical replicate failure plus transcription error.)'],
 ev:'Results §6.5.',
 trap:'Do not call it variability. Variability is a property of biology; this is a property of our bench. Saying which one it is is the whole answer.',
 lit:['lazic18']},

{id:'RS20',kind:'results',topic:'ours',lvl:2,
 q_en:'Your Western blot shows a band on 18 August and nothing on 23 August. What does that establish?',
 q_zh:'你們的西方墨點8月18日有帶、8月23日沒有。這建立了什麼？',
 claim:'That the 23 August culture carried no detectable ACC deaminase, so the sprays from that batch were not a protectant treatment. It establishes presence and absence, nothing more.',
 moves:['One sharp band at about 41 kilodaltons in the 18 August lane; the 23 August lane is blank. That is the batch we had sprayed on plants.',
        'What it supports: the antigen was below detection in that batch. That is enough to void the experiment, which is what we did.',
        'What it cannot support: activity, concentration, or whether the protein was folded. A band is immunoreactive material, and an enzyme assay is a different measurement that we do not have for any applied batch.',
        'What came out of it is a gate: nothing touches a plant now without a blot in front of it.'],
 stat:'One band at about 41 kDa on the 24 August blot; the fractionation blot places the secreted signal at 35 to 40 kDa. Two blots, two apparent masses, no loading control on the fractionation.',
 unit:'One blot. A blot is a presence or absence call at a detection limit we have not characterised, so "no detectable" is the correct phrase and "none" is not.',
 holds:['That set 8 is void and that we found it ourselves.',
        'The protocol gate, which is a contribution another team can adopt.'],
 fails:['Any quantity. A band is not a concentration without a standard curve. (No quantification, no loading control.)',
        'Any activity. (No enzyme assay on any applied batch.)'],
 ev:'Results §6.5 and §7.4.',
 trap:'If asked the mass, say "about 41 kilodaltons on the 24 August blot, 35 to 40 on the fractionation" and not one number. Two blots gave two values and pretending otherwise is the easiest catch in the session.'},

{id:'RS21',kind:'results',topic:'ours',lvl:3,
 q_en:'Your secretion evidence is one blot with bands in the supernatant and blank pellets. Is that enough?',
 q_zh:'你們分泌的證據是一張墨點，上清有帶、沉澱沒有。這夠嗎？',
 claim:'It is consistent with secretion and it does not exclude lysis, because the control that separates them was not run.',
 moves:['What we have: three fractions side by side on 2 August, with the 35 to 40 kilodalton signal in the supernatant lanes only. It is the only fractionation evidence that the tag does what it was chosen to do.',
        'What it does not exclude: cells that broke and released cytoplasmic contents into the medium. A blank pellet is consistent with that too, if the lysed cells are no longer in the pellet.',
        'The control that separates them is a cytoplasmic marker probed in the same lanes. If a protein that should never be secreted appears in the supernatant, the signal is lysis.',
        'And there is no loading control on this blot, so the lanes cannot be compared quantitatively even for the band we do see.'],
 stat:'One blot, three fractions, no loading control, no cytoplasmic marker, n = 1.',
 unit:'One blot from one culture. A single fractionation is an observation about that culture on that day.',
 holds:['That secreted material carrying the expected size exists in at least one preparation.'],
 fails:['Secretion as a mechanism. (No lysis control.)',
        'Any comparison of band intensity between lanes. (No loading control.)'],
 ev:'Results §7.4, the 2 August blot.',
 trap:'Volunteering the lysis control is what makes this answer. A judge with a protein background asks for it within two sentences, so get there first.'},

{id:'RS22',kind:'results',topic:'assay',lvl:3,
 q_en:'In set 4 your 1.0 millimolar no-salt control read lower than your 100 millimolar salt control. What does that do to the rest of that plate?',
 q_zh:'第4組裡，1.0 mM的無鹽對照比100 mM的加鹽對照還低。這對那一皿其他資料有什麼影響？',
 claim:'It invalidates the plate as a comparison, not only the arm that inverted. Every other number on it loses its reference.',
 moves:['The inversion is impossible biologically: an unstressed plant cannot carry less chlorophyll than a salt-damaged one at the same age, unless the measurement or the handling failed.',
        'Because the comparison on a plate runs through the plate\'s own controls, a failed control removes the baseline for every arm that shares it. The right action is to void the plate, and the wrong action is to drop the one bad arm and keep the rest.',
        'Set 4 also had its three readouts disagree: root length favoured trehalose and fresh weight did not, and 0.5 mM gave the highest chlorophyll.',
        'Readouts disagreeing is interesting when the controls hold. When the controls do not hold, disagreement is just the noise showing.'],
 stat:'Set 4 was the first with vertical plates and three readouts. One plate per group; three readouts, one inverted internal control.',
 unit:'Plate. An internal control failure is a plate-level event, so the unit that fails is the unit of inference.',
 holds:['The design lesson: the controls are read before the treatments, every time.'],
 fails:['The three-readout comparison in set 4. (Internal control failure.)'],
 ev:'Plants §4.1 set 4.',
 trap:'The instinct is to rescue the good-looking arms from a bad plate. Say out loud that the plate is the unit, so the whole plate goes.',
 lit:['lazic18']},

{id:'RS23',kind:'results',topic:'assay',lvl:2,
 q_en:'If a treated arm reads higher than your unstressed control, what are you looking at?',
 q_zh:'如果某個處理組比無逆境對照還高，你看到的是什麼？',
 claim:'One of three things, and they are separable by a dose series: a normalisation artefact, a low-dose stimulation, or a measurement error.',
 moves:['First check the denominator. If the figure is per gram of fresh weight, a lighter arm reads higher without being healthier. Ours did exactly this in two platforms.',
        'Second, low-dose stimulation is a documented pattern for chlorophyll specifically: across 177 dose responses, mild stress raises chlorophyll and severe stress lowers it. So an arm above its own control is not automatically an error.',
        'Third, a measurement error, which in our hands means a transposed column pair or a failed extraction. We have one documented instance of each.',
        'The test that separates them is a dose series on the same plate set. Stimulation is dose-dependent and reverses at higher dose; an artefact is not.'],
 stat:'Agathokleous and colleagues: median stimulation 150 to 160 per cent of control within a time window, settling to 120 to 130 per cent. Our own 0.5 mM trehalose arm gave the highest chlorophyll in set 4.',
 unit:'Whatever the figure normalised to. Answer the denominator question before the biology question.',
 holds:['That we check the denominator before we interpret a direction.'],
 fails:['Reading an above-control value as a benefit without the dose series. (Hormesis and ratio denominator are both live alternatives.)'],
 ev:'Plants §4.1 set 4, Results §6.4.',
 trap:'An arm that beats the healthy control looks like a win and is usually a denominator. Do not accept it, and do not dismiss it either — name the three candidates.',
 lit:['agathokleous20']},

{id:'RS24',kind:'results',topic:'model',lvl:2,
 q_en:'Your stress index fixes its weights in advance. What does that buy, and what does it not?',
 q_zh:'你們的逆境指標權重事先固定。這買到什麼？又沒買到什麼？',
 claim:'It removes one analytical degree of freedom. It does not create replication, fix a denominator, or produce a result.',
 moves:['What it buys: the weights cannot be tuned until the data looks good. Fixing them before looking is a small pre-registration, and it is why the index is worth having at all.',
        'The second thing it buys: the index is computed from raw values and not from already-normalised ones, which keeps the fresh-weight denominator out of it.',
        'What it does not buy: three readouts combined into one score is still one score from one plate. Combining does not add units.',
        'And we have not published an index value for any treatment. It is the method we would use, not a result we hold.'],
 stat:'Index defined as 1.0 at the matched unstressed control. Three readouts, weights fixed before analysis, computed from raw values.',
 unit:'Unchanged by the index. One plate stays one plate however many readouts it carries.',
 holds:['The method, as a contribution, with the pre-fixing rule attached.'],
 fails:['Any index value as a result. (None published; no replication to compute it over.)'],
 ev:'Plants §7.2.',
 trap:'Presenting the index as a result is the trap. It is a method with a pre-registration rule, and that is a smaller claim and a defensible one.'},

{id:'RS25',kind:'results',topic:'assay',lvl:3,
 q_en:'Over your whole salt range, where can your root readout actually detect a rescue?',
 q_zh:'在你們整個鹽度範圍裡，根長這個讀值到底在哪裡看得見救援？',
 claim:'Only around 100 mM, and even there the smallest resolvable difference is 6.4 millimetres against a mean of 11.',
 moves:['The detectable differences across the ladder are 3.90, 9.32, 6.43 and 1.34 millimetres at 0, 75, 100 and 150.',
        'At 75 mM the threshold is 9.32 mm against a mean of 34.77, so a rescue has to move the root by 27 per cent before we would see it. The spread there is the widest in the set.',
        'At 150 mM the threshold is small in absolute terms and the mean is smaller still, so it is a floor.',
        'That leaves 100 mM, where a detectable rescue is 6.43 mm on a mean of 11.00 — about 58 per cent of the stressed mean, or a recovery to roughly a sixth of the unstressed root. This is the honest sensitivity of our screen, and it is why a 3.71 mm result was never going to settle anything.'],
 stat:'Minimum detectable differences at power 0.80, alpha 0.05, n = 9 per arm: 3.90, 9.32, 6.43, 1.34 mm. As a fraction of the stressed mean: 5%, 27%, 58%, 99%. Pooled SDs: 2.96, 7.06, 4.87, 1.02 mm.',
 unit:'Computed at the seedling level, so every threshold above is the optimistic case. With the plate as the unit the thresholds do not exist.',
 holds:['A sensitivity statement for our own screen, which is the thing another team most needs before copying it.'],
 fails:['Any null from this screen read as absence of an effect. (Underpowered across the whole range.)'],
 ev:'Computed from the set 6 means and standard deviations in Plants §1.3 and Results §6.1.',
 trap:'This answer is uncomfortable and it is the one that ends a line of questioning. A team that can state its own detection limit is not guessing.',
 lit:['halsey25','nakagawa07']},

{id:'RS26',kind:'results',topic:'ours',lvl:3,
 q_en:'Do you have activity data on your protectants or not? Your pages seem to say both.',
 q_zh:'你們到底有沒有保護劑的活性資料？你們的頁面好像兩種說法都有。',
 claim:'No batch that was applied to a plant has an activity number behind it. Other pages do report an activity, from a different sample.',
 moves:['Scope it precisely: in the wet lab archive there is no ACC deaminase activity assay, no ELISA and no kinetics for any preparation that reached a plant. Everything labelled ACCD in the plant data is a phenotype test.',
        'Measurement and Biomanufacturing do report a permeate specific activity. That is a reactor sample, not a sprayed batch, and the two cannot be joined.',
        'There is also one DNPH estimate, and it sat below the assay\'s limit of quantification and was not repeated. Below the limit it is a direction, not a number.',
        'So the sentence is: no applied batch has an activity measurement. Not "we have no activity data", which our own other pages contradict within a minute.'],
 stat:'One DNPH estimate below the limit of quantification, unreplicated. No activity assay on any plant-applied batch.',
 unit:'The batch. Activity is a property of a preparation on a day, so an activity number from another sample does not transfer to the batch that was sprayed.',
 holds:['The scoped denial, which survives cross-examination against our other pages.',
        'The named gap: a powered rescue at 75 to 100 mM with a batch that has an activity number behind it.'],
 fails:['Any link between a plant result and a measured enzyme activity. (No measurement on the applied sample.)'],
 ev:'Results §6.5, §9. Read the Red lines page before answering this one.',
 trap:'A flat denial is the error here, not an overclaim. Scope the denial to the applied batch and name the page that holds the other number.'},

/* ── reading the page as a whole ── */
{id:'RS27',kind:'results',topic:'strategy',lvl:3,
 q_en:'Your Results page is mostly negative. What does it positively establish?',
 q_zh:'你們的Results頁幾乎都是負面的。它正面建立了什麼？',
 claim:'Four things, and each one is a measurement or a method another team can use.',
 moves:['A calibrated stress assay: a monotone four-point dose response with non-overlapping intervals, and a stated detection limit of about 6.4 millimetres at 100 mM. Almost no team publishes its own detection limit.',
        'A three-timing design, which is the experiment that decides whether production should be triggered by a forecast at all. Very few teams run it.',
        'Two failure rules that cost us sets and are now protocol: enter hydroponics from agar, and blot every batch before it touches a plant.',
        'And a measurement rule with the arithmetic behind it: never normalise to a denominator the treatment moved, demonstrated twice in two platforms.'],
 stat:'One clean dose response, four timing trials, two documented protocol gates, two platforms showing the same normalisation reversal.',
 unit:'Three of the four are methods, so the unit question does not apply to them. That is precisely why they are the durable part.',
 holds:['All four, as contributions.'],
 fails:['Any protectant efficacy claim. We do not make one, in any direction.'],
 ev:'Results §9 and the Contribution page, which carries the protocol as a PDF.',
 trap:'Listing everything the project did is the wrong answer. Four items, each executable by someone else, and stop.'},

{id:'RS28',kind:'results',topic:'strategy',lvl:3,
 q_en:'Which single number on your Results page is most likely to be wrong, and how would you check it in an hour?',
 q_zh:'Results頁上哪一個數字最可能是錯的？你會怎麼在一小時內查它？',
 claim:'The trial 4 chlorophyll table, and the check is to recompute every row from the raw optical densities.',
 moves:['Why that one: it carries the largest effect in our plant data, it uses a different acetone concentration and wavelength pair from the rest of the project, and one arm on the plate has an impossible internal control.',
        'The hour: pull the raw OD645 and OD663 columns, or 649 and 665 for that sheet, and recompute all twelve arms with the coefficients the sheet claims to use. Then recompute them with the columns swapped. If the broken arm lands on the ladder under the swap, it is a transposition.',
        'Second check in the same hour: re-trace twenty roots from the stored day-6 images, blind to the condition. The lengths are hand-traced in ImageJ, so tracer bias is the obvious failure mode and it is testable from existing files.',
        'Neither check needs plants, which is why they fit in an hour. Anything else needs three weeks of growth.'],
 stat:'Trial 4 is the only dataset in the project using 95 per cent acetone at 649 and 665 nm with Lichtenthaler coefficients; the page method is 80 per cent acetone at 645 and 663 nm.',
 unit:'The check is on the computation, not the biology, so it has no unit problem and that is why it is worth doing first.',
 holds:['An audit plan for our own strongest number.'],
 fails:['Nothing — this is a method answer.'],
 ev:'Plants §1.1 and Results §6.2 for the two wavelength conventions; Results §6.3 for the documented transposition in the heat sheet.',
 trap:'Naming a trivial number to look safe reads as evasion. Name the number you most want to be true, and give the check that could take it away from you.'}
];
